What:		/sys/bus/acpi/devices/.../path
Date:		December 2006
Contact:	Zhang Rui <rui.zhang@intel.com>
Description:
		This attribute indicates the full path of ACPI namespace
		node associated with the device.  For example, \_SB_.PCI0.
		This file does not exist for device that is created for
		ACPI fixed hardware features (power button and sleep
		button).

What:		/sys/bus/acpi/devices/.../modalias
Date:		July 2007
Contact:	Thomas Renninger <trenn@suse.de>
Description:
		This attribute indicates the PNP IDs of the device.  That
		is acpi:HHHHHHHH:[CCCCCCC:].  Where each HHHHHHHH or
		CCCCCCCC contains device's PNPID (_HID or _CID).

What:		/sys/bus/acpi/devices/.../hid
Date:		April 2005
Contact:	Linux ACPI developers <linux-acpi@vger.kernel.org>
Description:
		This attribute indicates the hardware ID (_HID) of the
		device.  For example, PNP0103.  When describing a platform,
		use of any _HID objects is optional.  However, a _HID
		object must be used to describe any device that will be
		enumerated by OSPM.

What:		/sys/bus/acpi/devices/.../description
Date:		October 2012
Contact:	Lance Ortiz <lance.ortiz@hp.com>
Description:
		This attribute indicates the string (_STR) of the device.
		It may be used by OSPM to provide information to an end
		user.

What:		/sys/bus/acpi/devices/.../adr
Date:		October 2012
Contact:	Lv Zheng <lv.zheng@intel.com>
Description:
		This attribute indicates the address (_ADR) of the device.
		This object must be used when specifying the address of
		any device on a bus that has a standard enumeration
		algorithm.

What:		/sys/bus/acpi/devices/.../uid
Date:		October 2012
Contact:	Lv Zheng <lv.zheng@intel.com>
Description:
		This attribute indicates the unique ID (_UID) of the
		device.  This identification object is optional, but is
		required when the device has no other way to report a
		persistent unique device ID.

What:		/sys/bus/acpi/devices/.../sun
Date:		November 2012
Contact:	Yasuaki Ishimatsu <isimatu.yasuaki@jp.fujitsu.com>
Description:
		This attribute indicates the slot user number (_SUN) of the
		device.  This object is used by OSPM UI to identify slots
		for the user.

What:		/sys/bus/acpi/devices/.../eject
Date:		December 2006
Contact:	Patrick Mochel <mochel@linux.intel.com>
Description:
		Writing 1 to this attribute will trigger hot removal of
		this device.  This file exists for every device that has
		_EJ0 method.

What:		/sys/bus/acpi/devices/.../real_power_state
Date:		January 2013
Contact:	Rafael J. Wysocki <rafael.j.wysocki@intel.com>
Description:
		This attribute indicates the device' real power state by
		obtaining power state before indicating to the user space.
		This file exists for every device that has _PRx method.

What:		/sys/bus/acpi/devices/.../power_state
Date:		January 2013
Contact:	Rafael J. Wysocki <rafael.j.wysocki@intel.com>
Description:
		This attribute indicates the device' current power state
		cached in the OSPM.  This file exists for every device that
		has _PS0 method.

What:		/sys/bus/acpi/devices/.../resource_in_use
Date:		January 2013
Contact:	Rafael J. Wysocki <rafael.j.wysocki@intel.com>
Description:
		This attribute indicates the current state of each ACPI
		power resource.
Users:
		Userspace applications (powertop in particular) use this
		attribute file to observe some properties of power
		resources for diagnostics purposes.
